# Compute Mann's U statistic for median TTCC with and without parks
mann <- wilcox.test(pct_tree_parks ~ has_park, data = phx_bg_all)

mann_u <- formatC(mann$statistic, format = "f", digits = 0)
mann_p <- ifelse(mann$p.value < 0.001, "0.001",
                 formatC(mann$p.value, format = "f", digits = 3))

# Compute Wilcoxon's V statistic for median PTCC with and without parks
wilcox <- wilcox.test(phx_bg_parks$pct_tree_parks, phx_bg_parks$pct_tree_wo_parks, paired = T)

wilcox_v <- formatC(wilcox$statistic, format = "f", digits = 0)
wilcox_p <- ifelse(wilcox$p.value < 0.001, "0.001",
                   formatC(wilcox$p.value, format = "f", digits = 3))
```
:::


Percent total tree canopy cover (TTCC) is the amount of tree canopy cover in a block group including the tree cover provided by public parks. Across the entire city, the 176 block groups that contain parks have a median TTCC of 10.95, while the 841 without parks have a median TTCC of 11.17. This difference is non-significant (Mann-Whitney *U* = 78897, *p* = 0.168), suggesting that parks are evenly distributed across the canopy spectrum rather than concentrated in already-green neighborhoods.

Percent private tree canopy cover (PTCC) is the tree cover in a block group excluding the tree cover provided by public parks. TTCC and PTCC were compared in the 176 block groups that contain parks to determine whether the parks significantly contribute to block group-level tree canopy cover in Phoenix. The median TTCC was 10.20%, while PTCC was higher at 10.95% . This difference was statistically significant (Wilcoxon signed-rank test, *V* = 11271, *p* \< 0.001), confirming that parks meaningfully contribute to tree canopy cover when they are present in a neighborhood.

### Regression analysis of tree canopy cover

Based on the OLS model, percent Hispanic population, percent Black population, and ADI rank were all negatively correlated with PTCC, significant at the *p* \< 0.001 level (see @tbl-regress). For every 1% increase in the Hispanic population, private tree canopy cover within a block group is expected to decrease by 3.5%; a 1% increase in the population of Black residents is associated with a 7.3% decrease in PTCC; and a 1-point increase in the ADI rank is associated with an 8.5% decrease in PTCC.

Residual diagnostics for the PTAS outcome indicated substantial right skew, so a square root transofrmation was applied prior to regression analysis. The percentage of Hispanic residents in a block group is not significantly associated with PTAS, but there was a significant positive association between PTAS and both Black resident percentage (*p* = 0.002) and ADI rank (*p* \< 0.001; see @tbl-regress).

These findings suggest that while neighborhoods with a higher Black population and lower socioeconomic status have lower tree canopy cover provided by trees on private property, street trees, and trees in private parks, the public parks near these neighborhoods provide greater access to tree canopy cover than in neighborhoods with lower Black populations and higher socioeconomic status.


::: {.cell .hidden}

```{.r .cell-code .hidden}
# Create weight matrix
phx_bg_pw <- readRDS("phx_bg_pw.rds")
nb8 <- knn2nb(knearneigh(phx_bg_pw, k = 8))
nbw8 <- nb2listw(nb8, style = "W")

# Global Moran's I for tree canopy
moran_canopy <- moran.mc(phx_bg$ols_canopy_resid, nbw8, nsim = 999)

moran_c_I <- formatC(moran_canopy$statistic, format = "f", digits = 3)

# Global Moran's I for park tree access
moran_access <- moran.mc(phx_bg$ols_access_resid, nbw8, nsim = 999)

moran_a_I <- formatC(moran_access$statistic, format = "f", digits = 3)
```
:::


Global Moran's *I* was significant (*p* \< 0.001) for both the PTCC and PTAS residuals from the OLS models. The PTCC index was 0.221, indicating moderate spatial clustering of private tree canopy values, and the PTAS index was 0.418, indicating strong spatial clustering of park tree access scores.

Spatial error model results are presented in @tbl-regress along with OLS estimates for comparison. Similar to the OLS model, all three predictor variables are negatively correlated with PTCC, indicating that block groups with larger Hispanic populations, larger Black populations, or higher ADI rank are more likely to have lower private tree canopy cover.

The spatial error model for the square-root transformed PTAS also indicates that there is no correlation between park tree access and Hispanic population percent. The coefficient of 0.019 for the Black population percent (significant only at the *p* \< 0.1 level) suggests that there may be a slight increase in park tree access as the Black population increases. The significant positive relationship between PTAS and ADI rank indicates that the park tree access score increases as socioeconomic status decreases.

The lambda ($\lambda$) term indicates how neighboring observations influence one another's errors. The positive $\lambda$ in both spatial error models indicates that unmeasured error in one block group significantly impacts the errors in adjoining block groups for both PTCC and PTAS.


